    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Natural combustion of hydrocarbons in narrow sub-vertical zones at Orcutt Oil field caused extensive alteration of the surrounding siliceous mudstone. Toward the core of the combustion zones, joint trace length strongly decreases, and the ratio of joint aperture to joint length increases. Significant volume loss and contraction fracturing related to the combustion heating resulted in brecciation and collapse, overprinting the preexisting regional jointing in the zones of highest alteration.\r\n\r\nBased on XRD characterization of distinct mineral assemblages in the alteration zones, estimates of alteration temperatures were assigned each zone. Finite difference thermal inversion of the inferred temperatures suggests pseudo-steady state conductive heat flow with a central temperature of ~950° C and a heating duration on the order of 5 years, indicative of a stationary zone of combustion, supplied by migrating fuel.\r\n\r\nOn a map and reservoir scale, the location of combustion zones appears to be controlled by faults that serve as a pathway from the hydrocarbon source to the surface. On the outcrop scale, the symmetry of the alteration pattern appears unaffected by the structural anisotropy of the joint set. However, structural features such as joints influence fluid and heat flow at the small scale in the outer zones.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
